---
// Lesson 3.3a — Fire Rate: Cooldowns & Time
// The lastAttackTime pattern: timers as state.
import Lesson from '../../layouts/LessonLayout.astro';

const body = `<h1>3.3a — Fire Rate: Cooldowns &amp; Time</h1>

<p class="lesson-lead">
    The turret can shoot — but if "in range" is the only condition, it
    fires a laser every frame: sixty bullets a second. Today: the
    cooldown, the pattern behind every rate limit in every game you
    have ever played.
</p>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="activity basic-app">
    <div class="activity-header">
        <span class="activity-label">Predict First</span>
        <span class="activity-time">~10 min</span>
    </div>

    <p>Read the turret's Update with the cooldown restored:</p>
    <pre><code>[SerializeField] private float attackCooldown = 2f;
private float lastAttackTime = 0f;
...
if (distanceToPlayer &lt;= maxAttackRange &amp;&amp; Time.time &gt;= lastAttackTime + attackCooldown)
{
    AttackPlayer();
    lastAttackTime = Time.time;
}</code></pre>

    <p>Predict without running: first frame in range — what is
    lastAttackTime, and does the turret fire instantly or after 2
    seconds? Then you dodge out and back in within the 2 seconds: does
    it fire again immediately or make you wait? Why does a
    <em>private</em> field with no [SerializeField] remember anything
    at all between frames?</p>

    <div class="socratic">
        <h3>Socratic</h3>
        <p>
            Update runs fresh sixty times a second, but lastAttackTime
            persists. Where does the value live between runs, and what
            would happen to the cooldown if fields reset with every
            frame like local variables do?
        </p>
    </div>
</div>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="activity basic-app">
    <div class="activity-header">
        <span class="activity-label">Build Lab</span>
        <span class="activity-time">~25 min</span>
    </div>

    <p><strong>Step 1 — see the laser.</strong> Temporarily change the
    condition to distance only:</p>
    <pre><code>if (distanceToPlayer &lt;= maxAttackRange)
{
    AttackPlayer();
    lastAttackTime = Time.time;
}</code></pre>

    <p>Press Play and step in range. That wall of projectiles is a
    frame-rate gun: one bullet per frame. Frame-rate-dependent behavior
    is the same bug as movement without deltaTime — the machine's
    speed becomes the game's rules. Restore the full condition and
    note that <code>&amp;&amp;</code> means BOTH must be true: a
    <strong>compound conditional</strong>, your first real one.</p>

    <p><strong>Step 2 — read the timer out loud.</strong>
    <code>Time.time</code> is seconds since the scene started: 0.0,
    1.6, 61.25... The condition asks "have at least 2 seconds passed
    since lastAttackTime?" — a countdown written as arithmetic. Change
    the <code>2f</code> to <code>5f</code> in the Inspector mid-game:
    the turret visibly slows. You have tuned a timer with the same
    workflow as tuning a speed.</p>

    <p><strong>Step 3 — the cooldown is a state.</strong> The turret
    now has two behaviors — SHOOTING and WAITING — and a field that
    remembers which. That is the seed of every state machine; in
    <a href="4.5a.html">4.5a</a> the enemy grows the same seed into
    patrol/chase/attack.</p>

    <div class="bug-hunt">
        <h3>Bug Hunt: Timer surgery</h3>
        <ol>
            <li>Delete the <code>lastAttackTime = Time.time;</code> line.
                Why did the laser come back, even though the condition
                still exists?</li>
            <li>Start lastAttackTime at −1000 instead of 0. What changes
                in the first minute — and why does the choice of
                starting value matter?</li>
            <li>Swap the condition to <code>&lt;=</code> on the time
                check (Time.time &lt;= last + cooldown). Is the turret
                faster, slower, or identical — and by how much per
                shot?</li>
            <li>Set attackCooldown to 0.01. What did you build — and
                why is this WORSE than the Step 1 laser if the player
                has an old laptop?</li>
        </ol>
    </div>
</div>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="activity basic-app">
    <div class="activity-header">
        <span class="activity-label">Tinker</span>
        <span class="activity-time">~10 min</span>
    </div>

    <div class="tinker">
        <h3>Tinker: Rate design</h3>
        <ol>
            <li>Tune attackCooldown by playtest against a partner: what
                cooldown makes dodging a decision instead of a
                reflex?</li>
            <li>The enemy's damage has the same cooldown pattern
                (damageCooldown = 1.0 in EnemyController.cs — find it).
                Why should touching-damage ALSO have a cooldown, and
                what would standing inside a spike feel like without
                one?</li>
            <li>Hard: burst fire — three shots 0.1s apart, then the
                full cooldown. What would you need: one timer, or a
                counter as well? Sketch the fields you would add.</li>
        </ol>
    </div>

    <div class="checklist">
        <h3>Checkpoint — before moving on, you must be able to:</h3>
        <ul>
            <li>Write the cooldown pattern from memory: Time.time, last time, compound if, update-after</li>
            <li>Explain why the turret fired a laser and how a field fixes it</li>
            <li>Tune a cooldown live in the Inspector</li>
            <li>Connect the cooldown to "state" — the turret remembers</li>
        </ul>
    </div>
</div>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="resources">
    <h3>Reference Docs</h3>
    <p>Need a deeper explanation? These pages cover the same concepts:</p>
    <ul>
        <li><a href="../docs/unit-03/index.html">Unit 3: The Turret</a> — cooldowns and the fire path</li>
        <li><a href="../docs/reference/unity-csharp-quickref.html">Unity + C# Quick Reference</a> — Time.time, Time.deltaTime</li>
    </ul>
    <p>Next: <a href="3.4a.html">3.4a — Turret Damage, Tuning &amp; Playtest</a></p>
</div>`;
---
<Lesson id="3.3a" title="3.3a Fire Rate: Cooldowns &amp; Time — AP CS Principles" root=".." css="../style.css">
  <Fragment set:html={body} />
</Lesson>
